            "content": "## Problem\n\nRetrieval-only recall systematically under-fires. `search_conversations` requires the model to *decide* to call it, and a model that does not know a relevant chat exists has no reason to look. This is the honest weakness of the #198/#216 design, and it is not fixable inside the tool — the tool is never invoked.\n\nChatGPT's context assembly buys the missing signal by dumping ~40 recent conversations, with message fragments, into every turn unconditionally. That is why it feels omniscient, and it is also the single most expensive, most privacy-surprising, and most injection-exposed thing in the snapshot studied in [`docs/research/long-term-memory/2026-07-27-user-context-injection.md`](../blob/master/docs/research/long-term-memory/2026-07-27-user-context-injection.md) §6. llame should not copy it.\n\nThe middle path: inject **awareness**, retrieve **content**. A bounded list of recent chat titles with dates and ids — no message bodies — gives the model a reason to call `search_conversations` (it sees `Ear canal odor — 20 Jul` and now knows there is something to look up), while the content path stays RLS-scoped, auditable, and framed on arrival.\n\n## Outcome\n\nA capped, owner-scoped digest of recent chat titles and dates is rendered as a trusted typed part immediately before the triggering user message, appears in the effective-context receipt, is disableable by its owner, and demonstrably raises `search_conversations` fire rate and answer quality on an eval set — or is not shipped.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Titles, dates, and chat ids only. **No message bodies, snippets, or excerpts** — that boundary is the whole design, not a first-version limitation.\n- Rendered by a **server-owned renderer** on the `renderModelSwitchReminder` model (`chats/model-context-part.ts`): strict shape validation on authoring, escaping on substitution, and explicit framing prose no operator can edit away.\n- Positioned in the **same slot as the existing reminders** — `context-builder.ts:306`, immediately before the visible user text — not the system prompt. This is what keeps the system prompt and the whole history inside the cached prefix.\n- Framed as data, never instructions, inheriting the `conversation-checkpoint` language already shipped.\n- Owner-scoped at the datastore. The digest reads the owner's own chats only and must be unreachable through the public/shared-chat path, which has no owner identity.\n- Independently disableable by the owner, alongside the existing personalization toggles.\n- Enumerated in the owner-visible effective-context receipt. Anything injected that the receipt does not disclose reproduces exactly the defect that produced the source snapshot.\n- Decide and document: whether archived chats appear, whether the current chat is excluded, and what an untitled chat renders as (`chats.title` is nullable since migration `0010`, and titling is asynchronous — a chat can legitimately have no title when the digest is built).\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- [ ] **An eval decides this.** With the digest on, `search_conversations` fires more often *when it should* and answer quality improves on a query set including cross-chat references; false-positive tool calls on self-contained questions do not regress. A digest that does not clear this bar is not merged — sizing and the cap come from the eval, not from prose.\n- [ ] Rendered output contains no message content, only titles, dates, and ids, asserted by test.\n- [ ] A title containing instruction-shaped or tag-shaped text cannot escape its framing (reuse the tag-balance discipline from `instance-config/authored-text.ts` if titles need it; titles are model-generated, so they are lower risk than authored text but are not trusted).\n- [ ] The digest is absent for an unauthenticated/shared-chat read, and one owner's digest never contains another owner's chats.\n- [ ] The owner's receipt discloses the rendered digest verbatim.\n- [ ] With the toggle off, the block is omitted entirely — no residual framing prose, matching the three-level omission rule personalization already follows.\n- [ ] Prefix-cache behavior is unchanged for the system prompt and history: the digest sits after the compactable prefix, so a per-turn change invalidates only the tail.\n\n## Boundaries\n\nNo message bodies, no embeddings, no semantic ranking, no automatic content injection, no inferred memory, no new memory store, and no change to `search_conversations` itself. Ranking within the digest is recency only — recency is a ranking signal, never a retention or deletion signal.\n\n## Cross-links\n\n- #216 is the deliberate-recall proof; this issue is the **awareness** half of the same loop and is a natural on-ramp to it. It does not replace or close #216.\n- #198 owns temporal semantics, provenance, and recency ranking inside the tool. This issue adds nothing to the tool.\n- The system-prompt rail is deliberately rejected here on three independent grounds — trust escalation (conversation-derived text in the system role is an injection privilege-escalation primitive), snapshot churn (a per-turn digest mints a full-prompt row in an append-only table on every turn), and prefix-cache forfeiture (position-0 volatility forfeits caching the entire history, not just the block). Reasoning in §4.2/§6 of the research note; the shipped personalization capability is the *static* half of the same split and is not a precedent for this one.\n",
